  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Transition to a diversified economy: An inclusive, sustainable and just development approach for the Colombian coal mining regions</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">RWE, Uniper, Onyx Power and other European energy companies source coal from Colombia to generate electricity. As climate change policies accelerate the decline of coal use and production, Colombia&#x2019;s coal mining regions face significant socio-economic challenges. Employment, public revenues, and the local economies are at risk, underscoring the urgent need to create viable &#x2018;beyond coal&#x2019; economic alternatives and decent jobs. The Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD) expects companies to assess the social, economic and environmental consequences before disengaging from a region or business activity. Disengagement should only occur responsibly, with measures to mitigate negative impacts on workers and communities. Companies should plan an orderly exit, engage stakeholders and explore support or transition measures. The Fund for Responsible Business (FVO) project aligns with these OECD expectations by helping shape responsible disengagement and just transition efforts in Colombia&#x2019;s coal regions.Over the past three years, the Colombia Beyond Coal project has contributed to this transition by promoting economic diversification, strengthening due diligence in the coal supply chain, and fostering social dialogue on just transition. Key achievements include the following:&#x2022;	Policy breakthroughs with local voices at the table: Mine closure and just transition were integrated into Colombia&#x2019;s new Mining Law and National Development Plan, while labour law reforms now include protection and labour conversion measures for mine workers affected by decarbonisation. These advances were directly shaped by the input of mine workers and local communities, supported by project partners.&#x2022;	Workers&#x2019; Collective for a Just Transition: The project facilitated the creation of this coalition of major trade unions in the coal sector, which became a strong advocate for workers&#x2019; rights during the energy transition and a driving force behind the establishment of a legally binding tripartite dialogue mechanism between government, mining companies and trade unions.&#x2022;	Corporate commitments: European energy companies strengthened their human rights and due diligence policies, embedding just transition and responsible mine closure into procurement and sustainability frameworks. Through RECOSI &#x2013; the Responsible Commodities Sourcing Initiative &#x2013; they also collectively raise supply chain standards, promoting responsible mine closure and broader social and environmental improvements among mining companies.&#x2022;	Pilot projects for diversification: Initiatives in cocoa, coffee, livestock and renewable energy cooperatives have demonstrated promising opportunities for local livelihoods, with some already embedded in national policies and supported by government investment programmes for ecological restoration and sustainable diversification in the mining regions.&#x2022;	Worker empowerment: Former mine workers have established new cooperatives in agriculture and renewable energy services.  &#x2022;	International engagement: Project partners helped initiate discussions for a Just Energy Transition Partnership (JETP) between Colombia and the European Union (EU), potentially mobilising finance for diversification and green development. While challenges such as political polarisation and the vulnerable position of women and informal workers remain, the project advanced awareness, dialogue, and concrete action. It has contributed to shaping the conditions for a more inclusive and sustainable transition in Colombia&#x2019;s coal regions &#x2013; with lessons that may inspire other coal-producing regions.</narrative>
  </description>
